<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-9-17">
  <title>Resources for 9/17</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of a <em>real-valued function</em>. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain definitions related to real-valued functions, such as <em>increasing functions</em>, <em>decreasing functions</em>, <em>monotone functions</em>, <em>bounded above functions</em>, <em>bounded below functions</em>, <em>maximum and minimum values of functions</em>, <em>relative maxima</em>, <em>relative minima</em>, and <em>relative extrema</em>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove theorems that involve real-valued functions.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-increasing-decreasing-">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Increasing &amp; Decreasing Functions</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>increasing &amp; decreasing Functions</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="TjoTlVLfxo0" />

    <problem>
      <title>Increasing &amp; Decreasing Functions</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Define <m>f: (0,\infty) \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> by <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{x^2}</m>.  Prove that <m>f</m> is decreasing.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Define <m>f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> by <m>f(x)=x^3</m>.  Prove that <m>f</m> is increasing.  Hint:  When you compare <m>f(x)</m> and <m>f(y)</m>, you will want to consider what the signs of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For <m>f</m> a real-valued function, prove the following.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f</m> is increasing <m>\Rightarrow g=-f</m> is decreasing.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f</m> is decreasing <m>\Rightarrow g=-f</m> is increasing.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f</m> is increasing <m>\Rightarrow g(x)=f(-x)</m> is decreasing.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f</m> is decreasing <m>\Rightarrow g(x)=f(-x)</m> is increasing.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-increasing-bounded">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Bounded Functions</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>bounded functions</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="NR6kiB36mxY" />

    <problem>
      <title>Bounded Functions</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Determine whether each of the following functions are bounded above, bounded below, has a maximum value, or has a minimum value.  You may use calculus reasoning to justify your answers.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m>, <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m>, <m>f(x)=x^2-6x+5</m>.  Hint:  Complete the square.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f: {\mathbb R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m>, <m>f(x)=\frac{1}{x^2+1}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>f: I \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> be a function, and let <m>S</m> be the range of <m>f</m>.  Prove that <m>f</m> has a maximum value if and only if sup<m>(S) \in S</m>.  What is the maximum value of <m>f</m> in this case?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      No reading for this content.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>